Hi there,

Thank you for reaching out to us about this — I completely understand how concerning it must be to see 500 errors popping up on your API integration, especially since nothing changed on your end. I appreciate you flagging this so quickly.

Let me walk you through exactly what's going on and how we can get this resolved. Based on what you've described, it sounds like this could be related to a server-side issue or perhaps a compatibility factor with the Python SDK v2.3. Rest assured, we're here to help you get to the bottom of it.

Here are the steps I'd recommend to diagnose and resolve the issue:

1. **Check the API status page** — Head over to status.api.example.com to see if there are any reported incidents, maintenance windows, or degraded performance that started yesterday. Also take a look at the SDK changelog for v2.3 to confirm your version hasn't been deprecated, and check if v2.4 or later includes fixes for recent server-side changes.

2. **Reproduce with verbose logging** — Enable DEBUG-level logging on your SDK to capture the full request/response cycle (headers, endpoint URL, response body with error codes or correlation IDs). You can also run `curl -v https://api.example.com/endpoint` independently to determine whether the error is SDK-specific or also affects raw HTTP calls.

3. **Escalate with full context if needed** — If the issue persists after confirming server status and SDK version, capture the exact timestamps of failures, the correlation ID from the 500 response body, your SDK version and Python version (`python --version`), and a minimal reproduction script. In the meantime, consider implementing retry logic with exponential backoff (starting at 1s, then 2s, 4s, up to 8s) to handle transient server errors gracefully.

If you have any questions about these steps or need further assistance, please don't hesitate to reply. We're here to help you every step of the way!

Best regards,
The Support Team

